 find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(s-2)$ is $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $1-s$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component ${s}^{2}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/91b196d5e312c4242a493f65abad556aa7f689640b09e0c51bc9124681abb48a.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (2,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162652/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162652"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-160789"] :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty )$ .\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{1+s}$ is  $s\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/3ac2933afc460e42ad5bffb8b01b9fc3ff9275ab3bcf2ac3ab710a8094b36637.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is\n\n$\\,$ $s\\in (-1,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162646/selection/b6d9fa1801779ca99866302b754e71f23c37af64d9c2754284